I found a viable native Track B family that avoids the orbit/diversity failure. It uses the paper’s Definition 2.1/2.2 directly: recover the coefficients of a projective point–hyperplane codeword from its values on a projective frame. Generic exact Gaussian elimination is \(O(n^3)\); the selected frame restriction is a permuted \(J-I\) matrix, so recognizing the total-sum invariant reduces recovery to \(2n\) field operations. The random target values make seeds structurally distinct, while projective-coordinate and input-order relabellings are quotiented out. I’m implementing this and will let the required oracle runs decide whether the no-tool gap is real.
STEP 0 supports a Track B build based on the paper's native incidence-code definition; the explicit algorithm is exact Gaussian elimination, while the constructed frame restriction admits a short invariant route.
